<title>UK: World drug sales up 6 percent in H1 - IMS survey. [CORRECTED 12:31 GMT]</title>
<headline>World drug sales up 6 percent in H1 - IMS survey. [CORRECTED 12:31 GMT]</headline>
<dateline>LONDON 1996-09-30</dateline>
<text>
<p>Pharmaceutical sales in ten of the world's leading markets rose 6 percent year-on-year in local currency terms in the first six months of 1996, healthcare information specialist IMS International said on Monday.</p>
<p>The figures cover the top seven European markets, plus Japan, the USA and Canada. They do not include countries such as Brazil, which in sales terms would be within the top ten.</p>
<p>The fastest-growing markets in local currency terms were Italy, the UK and Spain, with growth of 12, 10 and nine percent respectively. By category, sales of blood agents grew by 16 percent and central nervous sytem drugs by 13 percent.  </p>
<p>IMS detailed retail pharmacy sales as follows, in billion dollars. Growth rates are in local currency terms.</p>
<p>			  Jan-June 96  Jan-June 95	Percent growth</p>
<p> (corrects headings from Jan-Feb 95 and Jan-Feb 94 respectively)</p>
<p> U.S.		   28.990	  27.129		  7</p>
<p> Canada		  1.838	   1.749		  3</p>
<p> Germany		 8.445	   8.278		  6</p>
<p> France		  7.568	   7.282		  5</p>
<p> Italy		   4.503	   3.818		 12</p>
<p> UK			3.227	   3.073		 10</p>
<p> Spain		   2.607	   2.367		  9</p>
<p> Netherlands	   1.021	   1.038		  3</p>
